//! The boot handoff (spec §12): the boot-handle record and the boot handle set.
//!
//! The three-word record a `Give` writes and a `BootHandleNext` hands back, the
//! kinds it can name, and the two constants the kernel's own handoff is written
//! in.
//!
//! Reached through the `sosabi` facade; see `lib.saw`.

// =============================================================================
// The boot-handle record (spec §12)
// =============================================================================
//
// `Process.BootHandleNext` answers with a record the kernel copies out, on the
// `Waiter.Wait` precedent and through the same funnel. Three machine words:
// which region this is, what kind of object was minted for it, and the handle.
//
// It is machine words, not doublewords, by the wait record's rule: a tag, a
// kind and a handle are all machine-word-shaped, so the layout follows the
// register. Only a time is a fixed 64 bits on every profile.
//
// The record is consumed by delivery, and there are two deliveries. The drain
// hands back the next undelivered record and advances; there is no rewind, no
// peek and no count op. A drained iterator answers `SosStatus.Drained` forever.
// That is a loop's termination check, never a fault: exhaustion is what a
// caller could not have known in advance and is the ordinary way the loop ends.
// The other delivery is `start(boot_tag:)`'s resolution: putting a record's
// word in the child's first argument register is a delivery like any other, so
// that record is spent and the drain skips it. One record, one delivery,
// whichever door it went out of, so "a child can never be handed one word
// twice" is a property of the kernel rather than an obligation on the child.
//
// The set is per-process, and there are two ways one gets filled: the kernel
// writes root's at boot, one record per row of the build-emitted region table,
// and a launcher writes a child's with `ProcessOp.Give`, one record per
// capability it hands across. Nothing about the record or the op distinguishes
// them; the kernel is the giver nobody gave to. The set freezes at `Start` (a
// give afterwards is a `BadState` fault), so a half-populated table is
// unrepresentable at the child's first instruction with no synchronization; the
// drain has no deadline, so a child that drains late drains correctly.

/// Words in a boot-handle record: tag, kind, handle.
public static BOOT_HANDLE_RECORD_WORDS: Int = 3

/// Where each lives. Named rather than written, on both sides of the boundary.
public static BOOT_HANDLE_TAG_WORD: Int = 0
public static BOOT_HANDLE_KIND_WORD: Int = 1
public static BOOT_HANDLE_HANDLE_WORD: Int = 2

/// Bytes in a boot-handle record: the minimum capacity a caller must give the
/// op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A function and not a `static`, for the reason `wait_record_bytes` gives:
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer.
public func boot_handle_record_bytes() -> Int {
    BOOT_HANDLE_RECORD_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}

/// What kind of object a boot-handle record names: the record's kind word.
///
/// Public API rather than kernel bookkeeping: the kernel writes this value and
/// userspace reads it, so one declaration has to serve both. It is deliberately
/// not `ObjType`, which is kernel-internal and renumberable: a boot handle's
/// kind crosses the trap boundary, so it is its own closed set with its own
/// stated numbers. The raw backing lets a kind be added without renumbering.
///
/// The set is exactly the givable kinds, and it is smaller than `ObjType` on
/// purpose. A kind reaches this enum when something can deliver one, which
/// means its default rights mint the universal `Transfer` bit:
/// `pool_rights()`, `memory_rights()`, `iomemory_rights()`, `process_rights()`,
/// `root_system_rights()`, `event_rights()`, `thread_rights()`,
/// `waiter_rights()`, `timer_rights()`, `interrupt_rights()` and the pipe sets.
/// A Clock handle cannot be given, and a case for one would be dead surface,
/// the same rule `ClockType` follows in declaring one domain. The kernel's
/// `boot_kind_of` is the one place the two enums meet, and it refuses what it
/// cannot spell rather than inventing a number.
///
/// `System` is the one a launch actually uses: it is what a child receives in
/// its first argument register, a masked sibling of root's own, which makes
/// every process bootstrap the same way (§12).
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the word it travels in, for the reason
/// `WaitTag` gives: userspace decodes it through `from(raw:)`, which is total.
///
/// The tag beside it is the giver's own word, handed back unread. For root's
/// boot set the kernel is the giver and the tags are region ordinals (row `n`
/// is tag `n`); for a child's set the launcher is the giver and the tag is
/// whatever it chose. The kernel knows nothing about either (which ordinal is
/// an image blob, which is destination RAM, which capability a child expects
/// under 7) because the tag is the identity, and identities are the only
/// cross-process vocabulary there is. Keeping the meaning in the launcher's
/// config is what makes the kernel's half of the launch flow the same code for
/// a hand-built system and a dynamic one.
///
/// A region table row mints the kind its kind column names: a Ram row a
/// `Memory` (an allocation), a Pool row a `Pool`, a Device row an `IoMemory`.
/// Root receives each through the same drain as everything else, and the
/// record's kind says which it is. Each is `Transfer`-able, because handing a
/// child its RAM, a budget to allocate from, or a driver its window is a
/// launcher's ordinary flow.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Mapping` case. A Mapping is not givable
/// (`mapping_rights()` withholds `Transfer` on purpose; see `MappingRight`), so
/// a case for one would be dead surface on the rule this set follows.
public enum BootHandleKind: UInt {
    case Memory = 0,
    case Process = 1,
    case System = 2,
    case IoMemory = 3,
    /// The pair's two ends. `pipe_inlet_rights()` and `pipe_outlet_rights()`
    /// mint `Transfer` because handing a child one end of a connection is the
    /// point of the pair: a launcher creates it and wires the two ends outward.
    case PipeInlet = 4,
    case PipeOutlet = 5,
    /// The one-shots. `pipe_reply_rights()` and `pipe_request_rights()` mint
    /// `Transfer` because §2.1 makes both one-shots transferable and calls it
    /// the delegation primitive: a server moves its obligation to a third
    /// party, who replies straight to the original client, and the payload
    /// never transits the middle process. So a give must be able to carry one,
    /// and a give's record must be able to spell it.
    case PipeReply = 6,
    case PipeRequest = 7,
    /// An Event. `event_rights()` mints `Transfer` so that a launcher can hand
    /// a child a notification (a producer end, through a keep mask) and a
    /// message can carry one.
    case Event = 8,
    /// A Thread. `thread_rights()` mints `Transfer` so that a thread's creator
    /// can hand its handle on, at boot or in a message, to whoever joins it.
    case Thread = 9,
    /// A Waiter and a Timer. `waiter_rights()` and `timer_rights()` mint
    /// `Transfer` so that a creator can hand either on, at boot or in a
    /// message.
    case Waiter = 10,
    case Timer = 11,
    /// An Interrupt. `interrupt_rights()` mints `Transfer` so that a launcher
    /// can bind a line and hand the Interrupt to the driver that services it,
    /// at boot or in a message. It has one name, so the give moves it.
    case Interrupt = 12,
    /// A Pool. `pool_rights()` mints `Transfer` so that a launcher can hand a
    /// child a byte budget of its own, at boot or in a message.
    case Pool = 13,
}

// The decoded record is not declared here. A wait answer is made of plain words
// (a key, a tag, a count), so one declaration serves the kernel that writes it
// and the process that reads it. A boot-handle answer carries a handle, and the
// point of the `sos` module's typed layer is that a handle a process holds is a
// `Memory` or a `Process` and never a word. So the decoded form is `sos`'s
// `BootHandle`, which this file could not name; what crosses is the three-word
// layout above, declared once, here.

// =============================================================================
// The boot handle set (spec §12)
// =============================================================================

/// The handle the kernel gives root for the System object. Root receives it in
/// the first argument register at entry rather than assuming it, so this
/// constant is the kernel's choice of what to mint, not a number userspace is
/// expected to know.
public static ROOT_SYSTEM_HANDLE: UInt = 1

/// The `stack_top` handed to a user entry that was granted no stack region.
public static NO_USER_STACK: UInt = 0
